In article <1990Jun2.173002.26255@cs.uoregon.edu> ghudson@cie.UUCP (Greg Hudson) writes:
>a "fur covering" or something, designed for some type of armor (a
>blessed one would fit any type of armor) that would make that type of
>armor (e.g. boots, suit, helmet) rustproof.  It could, obviously, be
>used only once.  I don't think it would be necessary to code in the
>ability to remove it from the armor once applied.


Close to a good idea!  Let's try to make it better!  How about a fairly rare
item, a can of (bear?) grease that can be used to protect armor and weapons
from _any_ kid of rust, for a limited period of time (nnnn moves).  It rubs
off as you walk around, so your chances of being successfully "rusted" go
up with time until some cutoff when they become 100% of the base chance of
the attack again.  If you don't find it, or don't use it in a timely fashion,
you rust as before.  It is low overhead, because it only has to be checked
during a rusting attack, not at every move.

One can covers exactly all the armor and weapons you then have with you. 
Like eating or counting gold, you have to stop and sit down to use it,
and if interrupted, only part of your stuff is protected, but the can is
lost in the struggle.

This adds another element of tension to the game as you hoard your only can
of "Dr. RustPruf Brand Bear Grease" hoping _to_ find a noble weapon or armor,
and _not to_ find a pool or rust monster.  Optionally, fighter types might
have a large chance of starting the game with a can of grease in the backpack.

Most of the code to do this kind of stuff (aging the rustproofing, interrupting
application, etc.) is already in the game, and might get generalized a bit
in the process of making this work.  Are there other goodies that should
follow this general pattern, and would enhance the game?

Definitely a 3.1 feature.  Which brings up a point.  The dev team has promised
to go out of business once the "last bug" is nailed in 3.0, and all the
platforms are working right.  Hard to blame them; this has been a huge
project,  the kind that beats you down into your socks, and the students
among them are all eminently hireable as successful big C programming project
task participants.

Question is, is anyone in line to pick up the maintenance and enhancement
tasks for the next cycle?  (That might well be two separate, but
communicating groups.)

The game has long passed the size where a port to an OOP Language seems in
order, to simplify maintenance and addition of similar features. C++, maybe?

